  "vulnerabilities": [
    {
      "cve": "CVE-2026-85644",
      "ids": [
        {
          "system_name": "SUSE CVE Page",
          "text": "https://www.suse.com/security/cve/CVE-2026-85644"
        }
      ],
      "notes": [
        {
          "category": "general",
          "text": "XS::Parse::Infix versions from 0.40 through 0.49 for Perl treat a number as an array reference.\n\nThe wrapper function XS::Parse::Infix generates for a list-associative infix operator checks whether arguments are array references, but it tests using SvRV() rather than SvROK(). SvRV() reads a union slot that only holds a referent once SvROK(sv) is true, so the guard never validates that it is a reference. For an IV or NV that slot holds the number itself, SvRV() returns the caller's value and SvTYPE() dereferences it at offset 12. This will generally result in a segmentation fault.\n\nAn application that hands the wrapper a list built from decoded input (for example, from JSON) lets whoever supplies a number in that list choose the address that the interpreter dereferences.\n\nAn ordinary string's byte 12 is rarely SVt_PVAV so the guard croaks by luck, but an attacker-crafted string carrying 0x0b there passes, and the buffer is then used as an AV head, with AvARRAY taken from bytes 16-23 and its entries pushed onto the Perl stack as live SVs.\n\nA simple proof-of-concept uses the zip operator:\n\n    use Syntax::Operator::Zip 'zip';\n\n    my @args = ([1], 2);\n    zip(@args);",
          "title": "CVE description"
        }
      ],
